  "text": "**Solution 3 has the correct final answer, 2.** I checked it against the structure of the problem, not against a source I could verify, so treat this as a reasoned judgment.\n\n- **Velocity:** Near the critical layer $y_c$, the stream function has a logarithmic term $(y-y_c)\\log|y-y_c|$. That gives $\\psi \\in H^{5/2-\\tau}$ and velocity $u=\\psi_y$ in $H^{3/2-\\tau}$. This matches the Lin–Zeng threshold for Couette-type flows in a channel, where vorticity sits in $H^{1/2-}$ and velocity in $H^{3/2-}$. So $s_v = 3/2$.\n- **Density:** The perturbation is $\\rho_1 \\sim \\bar\\rho'_c\\,\\psi/(U-c)$. Since $\\psi/(U-c)$ is of order $\\log|y-y_c|$, the density behaves like a log singularity and lies in $H^{1/2-\\tau}$ but not $H^{1/2}$. So $s_\\rho = 1/2$.\n- The sum is $3/2 + 1/2 = 2$.\n\n**Why the others fail:**\n- **Solution 1** assigns amplitude and width scalings ($\\epsilon$, $\\sqrt\\epsilon$) that are asserted rather than derived. Its velocity threshold of $5/2$ would also mean the velocity has more regularity than the Lin–Zeng $H^{3/2-}$ threshold for the homogeneous case, so its homogeneous-limit check does not hold up.\n- **Solution 2** uses $s_v = 1/2$, which is inconsistent with the $H^{3/2-}$ velocity threshold. Its mapping from a $\\ln|\\delta|$ streamfunction singularity to $u' = \\psi_y$ is also not handled correctly.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
